 Top of the Morning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 State attorneys general have become America's top antitrust cops, believing that federal regulators have become too quick to approve or settle the thorniest of deals.

 Not surprisingly, the Trump administration doesn't like that too much. Driving the news: Stanley Woodward, who oversees the Justice Department's antitrust division, sought to reassert federal primacy last week during a speech at Fordham Law School .

 To be clear, this wasn't a fire-and-brimstone rant. It was academic and measured, as befitting the setting, with Woodward acknowledging overlapping regulatory authorities. But it still took aim at state AGs he says engage in "gamesmanship" and "antitrust overenforcement." "When a merger harms local markets, we want the states in the room," Woodward said. "When it is national, we expect to lead." Zoom in: Woodward also took issue with those arguing that political appointees like himself are less qualified to make antitrust determinations than are career staff — arguing that both federal executive and state officials have voter-validated roles to play.

 This seemed to be a direct response to a speech at Georgetown earlier in the week by Colorado AG Phil Weiser, who is among those who challenged Paramount's pending deal for Warner Bros. Discovery, the Nexstar-Tegna merger, and Kroger's failed deal for Albertson's. State of play: All of this could come to a head in the aforementioned Paramount situation, with multiple reports that the states could be poised to settle.

 If California and other states settle with significant concessions — even over the objections of progressives like Lina Khan — then it may validate their more active posture. For example, the WSJ reports that one proposal would include Paramount investing $1.5 billion in California production. Bloomberg notes that Paramount might agree to a $30 million-per-film penalty if it "fails to make good on a promise to distribute 30 films per year in theaters." DOJ, meanwhile, signed off on the deal without any remedies and last week filed a statement of interest in favor of Paramount's request that the state AGs put up a $1.88 billion bond. It could view the settlement talks as an admission that the states' legal case is weak. If the states walk without a major trophy, it would help validate Woodward's argument that state AGs are choosing politics over policy. The bottom line: When everyone is in charge, no one really is. And that makes it much tougher for companies to plan future deals.

 
 
