 The BFD 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Nscale, a London-based AI hyperscaler, on Friday filed for its IPO.

 Why it's the BFD: This would be the first major AI listing since the safety debate exploded into the mainstream and Anthropic — a major Nscale customer — talked about "pacing the frontier."

 Nscale's S-1 also is a map of circular AI financing , with Nvidia as a featured player. By the numbers: Renaissance Capital estimates that Nscale's IPO could raise around $2 billion.

 The company reports a $1.02 billion net loss on $140.6 million in revenue for the first half of 2026, versus a $369 million net loss on $10.4 million in revenue for the year-earlier period. Nscale also reports over $103 billion of total contracted value, and has raised billions of VC dollars (plus lots of debt) — most recently this past spring at a $13.5 billion post-money valuation — from firms like Aker, Sandton Capital Partners, and Paladin Capital. The bottom line: "Spun off from a cryptocurrency mining operation in early 2024, Nscale quickly became one of the most prominent data center newcomers powering the AI boom as a so-called neocloud operator, renting out computing resources to companies developing AI." — Subrat Patnaik, Bloomberg 

 
 
